Summary

Many people assume that wealthy countries are automatically happier because they have higher GDP and higher wages. This seems logical, but it doesn’t always match reality. Our project looks more closely at whether richer countries with higher minimum wages are actually happier, and how these economic factors compare to social and health factors like life expectancy, social support, and freedom.

To study this, we used two datasets from Kaggle. The first comes from the World Happiness Report 2024 and includes each country’s happiness score along with information such as social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity, and perceptions of corruption. The second dataset, Countries of the World 2023, provides economic and demographic information such as GDP, minimum wage, unemployment rate, population, and tax indicators.

We cleaned each dataset separately by removing missing values, fixing inconsistent country names, and converting formatted numbers like GDP and minimum wage into actual numeric values. We also removed countries with incomplete or non-usable data so that the analysis would be consistent. For example, we removed Tajikistan because it was missing important happiness variables, and we noted Afghanistan as an outlier due to its unusually low happiness score.

After cleaning, we joined the two datasets using the country name. This gave us a combined dataset with 115 countries and 27 variables. Because our project focuses on minimum wage, we removed the 21 countries that do not have a national minimum wage law. Many of these countries use collective bargaining instead, which makes them hard to compare directly. After removing these cases, our analysis used 94 countries with complete data across happiness, minimum wage, GDP, and the main social and health indicators.

We then calculated correlations and ran a linear regression model. We found that factors such as social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, and minimum wage have much stronger relationships with happiness than GDP does. Total GDP had only a weak correlation with happiness, while social and health measurements were much more important. Our regression model explained over 80 percent of the differences in happiness across countries, and GDP was not a significant predictor once we controlled for the other variables.

Overall, our analysis suggests that being a wealthy country does not automatically make people happier. What matters more is how that wealth is used to support health, social connections, and personal freedom. By combining two different datasets, we were able to get a clearer picture of what influences national happiness beyond just GDP.

Data Profile 

We used two main datasets from Kaggle for this project. The first dataset is based on the World Happiness Report 2024. It includes each country’s happiness ladder score and several related variables such as social support, log GDP per capita, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity, and perceptions of corruption. These values come from the Gallup World Poll and other global data sources. The dataset is aggregated at the country level, so no individual-level data is involved.

The second dataset is Countries of the World 2023, which provides economic and demographic information from sources like the World Bank and United Nations. The variables we used from this dataset include nominal GDP, national minimum wage (reported in US dollars), life expectancy, population, unemployment rate, labor force participation, and tax indicators. This dataset is also aggregated at the country level.

We joined the two datasets using standardized country names after cleaning inconsistent entries. Before the join, we removed rows that did not represent actual sovereign countries. The integrated dataset initially contained 115 countries and 27 variables, combining happiness metrics with economic and demographic indicators. However, 21 countries did not have a national minimum wage, and since minimum wage is central to our research question, we excluded those from the analysis portion of the project. The final dataset used for analysis therefore includes 94 countries.

Our dataset represents a single point in time and does not track changes across years. Because of this, our findings are descriptive and correlational rather than causal.

Data Quality 

Before analyzing the data, we checked both datasets for missing values, inconsistencies, and formatting issues. In the World Happiness Report dataset, almost everything was complete except for Tajikistan, which was missing important happiness fields, so we removed it. Afghanistan’s score was extremely low, but since it was valid data, we kept it while noting that it was an outlier.

In the Countries of the World dataset, the main issue was that 21 countries did not have a national minimum wage. Since this is an essential variable for our research question, we did not try to estimate or fill in these values. Instead, we excluded those countries from the final analysis.

We also fixed formatting issues. For example, GDP and minimum wage were stored as strings with symbols like “$” and commas. We removed these characters and converted them to numeric values so they could be analyzed properly. We checked for any leftover text entries and treated blank cells as missing data.

Country names needed to be standardized because some names were written differently across the two datasets. We cleaned these so that each country would match correctly, and we removed any entries that were territories rather than full countries.

Overall, after cleaning, the final analysis dataset has no missing values in the variables we used for our models. All important variables are numeric and consistent across countries.

Findings 

Our findings focus on how happiness relates to economic, social, and health indicators. The happiest countries in the dataset tend to be high-income democracies with strong social systems, such as Finland, Denmark, and Iceland. Countries facing conflict or economic instability generally had much lower happiness scores. However, GDP alone did not explain these differences very well.

The correlation results showed that social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, and minimum wage all had strong positive relationships with happiness. GDP, on the other hand, had a very weak correlation with happiness. This means that while money might help indirectly, the things that seem to matter most are health, support networks, and personal freedom.

We also ran a multiple linear regression model. This model explained more than 80 percent of the differences in happiness across countries. In this model, social support, freedom, minimum wage, and life expectancy were all important predictors. GDP, however, was not significant after controlling for these other factors.

In short, our results suggest that simply increasing a country’s GDP is not the most effective way to increase happiness. Instead, improving social and health systems appears to have a much stronger connection to wellbeing.

Future Work 

There are several ways our project could be expanded. One improvement would be to use data across multiple years instead of just a single year. This would let us see how changes in GDP, minimum wage, or health systems relate to changes in happiness over time.

It would also be useful to include measures of inequality, because country averages do not show how happiness differs within a country. Some countries might have high average happiness but still have groups who are struggling.

Another extension would be to explore more detailed causal methods. Our project shows correlations, but it cannot prove cause and effect. With better data, we could look at policy changes or major economic shifts to study how they influence happiness.

We could also include more cultural or political variables, such as trust in government or levels of social capital, which might help explain differences between countries with similar GDP levels.

Finally, our workflow could be improved with more automation and clearer metadata. Creating scripts that handle the entire cleaning and merging process would make the project easier to reproduce, and adding more visualizations could help communicate results more clearly.

